[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not confirm any flapping of PC12’s default gateway on the LAN or instability of EIGRP on R12. Evidence on the LAN side (R12 eth1 toward PC12) shows a stable interface with no link state changes: “show interface eth1” reports Link ups: 0, Link downs: 0 (never), and ip -s link/ethtool show no errors or drops. From PC12, 100 rapid pings to the gateway 120.1.1.1 completed with 0% loss and sub-millisecond RTTs; ARP on both ends is healthy (PC12 sees 120.1.1.1 REACHABLE; R12 sees 120.1.1.10 with a normal neighbor state). PC12’s dmesg shows no carrier changes.

On the routing side, R12’s EIGRP adjacency is on eth0 to 112.1.1.1 (the core), not the PC12 LAN. FRR logs show the neighbor came up cleanly and there are no bounce messages; “show ip eigrp neighbors” is steady. Even with EIGRP enabled on the 120.0.0.0/8 network, there is no LAN neighbor to flap, so this cannot cause local default-gateway loss.

Localization: R12 eth1 (120.1.1.1/8) toward PC12 and R12’s EIGRP on eth0 are both stable. Root cause: No fault found on R12’s LAN interface or its EIGRP session.

Corrective fix: None on the gateway. If intermittent issues persist, capture evidence during an event (continuous ping to 120.1.1.1 and a tcpdump on R12 eth1), and investigate host-side NIC/VM pauses or upstream path issues unrelated to the local gateway. 
=== END ===